WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.800
Azure CLI output is often consumed by
later automation.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.872
A script may query JSON, extract a
resource ID, inspect a role assignment,

3
00:00:12.952 --> 00:00:19.267
select a deployment target, compare
configuration, write a file,

4
00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:23.294
or decide whether a later step should run.

5
00:00:23.374 --> 00:00:30.366
That output can include identifiers,
subscription details, endpoints,

6
00:00:30.446 --> 00:00:38.340
policy results, role assignments,
configuration values, and sensitive context.

7
00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.172
Structured parsing is safer than fragile
text assumptions.

8
00:00:44.252 --> 00:00:52.259
Scripts should parse expected JSON fields
deliberately and handle missing fields,

9
00:00:52.339 --> 00:00:58.090
empty responses, multiple matches, and
unexpected values.

10
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:05.613
JMESPath-style query concepts can be
useful defensively when they make field

11
00:01:05.693 --> 00:01:13.474
selection explicit, but the script still
needs to confirm that the returned value

12
00:01:13.554 --> 00:01:16.374
matches the intended target.

13
00:01:16.454 --> 00:01:24.348
Output files should go to approved
destinations with appropriate permissions and

14
00:01:24.428 --> 00:01:26.909
retention expectations.

15
00:01:26.989 --> 00:01:33.642
Generated JSON, deployment details, policy
results, role listings,

16
00:01:33.722 --> 00:01:41.616
and support bundles can reveal sensitive
operational context even when they do not

17
00:01:41.696 --> 00:01:43.614
contain raw tokens.

18
00:01:43.694 --> 00:01:50.573
A script should avoid writing sensitive
output into shared workspaces,

19
00:01:50.653 --> 00:01:56.968
broad CI/CD artifacts, or long-lived logs
without a clear reason.

20
00:01:57.048 --> 00:02:01.446
Logs should provide minimal useful
evidence.

21
00:02:01.526 --> 00:02:08.856
Good logs show high-level identity,
tenant, subscription, resource group,

22
00:02:08.936 --> 00:02:13.109
action, target category, time, and result.

23
00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:19.053
They avoid tokens, secrets, service
principal credentials,

24
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:25.674
sensitive role details, private data, and
excessive raw responses.

25
00:02:25.754 --> 00:02:33.873
Secure output handling lets automation
remain useful without turning logs and files

26
00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.660
into another exposure path.
